
Text: "The following illustrates how to define an abstract concept, in this case
       a set, that satisfies some axioms and has certain operations on it. In
       this case the operation is set membership \"in\" and \\abstract indicates
       that the operation is abstract. That is, its definition is dependent on
       the specific being defined.

       The Requires: section indicates capabilities and definitions that this
       definition also includes. In this case, for something to be a set it must
       have the capability to have an \"in\" specification that represents set
       membership.

       In addition, the Enables: section indicates capabilities that an object
       of this type has, not because they are there definitionally, but because
       they are also available. For example, if $x$ and $y$ is an element of a
       ring $R$ then the $+$ operation is part of the definition of the ring, so
       one can write $x + y$. However, we can also write $x - y$, *not* because
       the $-$ operation is part of the ring, but because it is an operation we
       get from the $+$ operation.

       The Requires: and Enables: sections allow you to clearly separate these
       two cases.

       In this case, the `Describes: X` uses the symbol `X` which means the
       shape of a set is unknown. Contrast this, to `Describes: {x__ : ...}`
       which says a set has a shape like `{x__ : ...}`.

       We don't want to require a set to have a particular shape. We want it to
       be abstract in that regard, but we want to allow write
       ```mlg-fragment
          X := {x_ : x_ is \real} is \set
       ```

       The from:capability: is how we communicate that in the case above, where an value of the form {y__ : ...} is used to introduce a set, then
       the abstract concept `x_ \"in\" X` means `x_ member_of Y`.

       Note that `member_of` is a keyword and `y member_of {x_ : x_ is \real}`,
       for example, resolves to `y is \real`.

       Next, this example illustrates the Documented:called: and Documented:description: sections"
Id: "8d820a04-5252-4ef2-9177-b1b328328197"


[\set]
Describes: X
satisfies: \axiom.of.empty.set
Requires:
. capability: x_ "in" X :-> \\abstract
Enables:
. from: Y ::= {y__ : ...}
  capability: x_ "in" X :-> x_ member_of Y
Documented:
. called: "set"
. description: "A set is the sole primitive object of the theory."
Id: "27f9dbd5-5b5e-4be3-b280-1afef8411f2d"


[\axiom.of.empty.set]
Axiom:
then:
. exists: X is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: Y is \set
    then:
    . not: Y "in" X
Id: "64ef9588-84e8-431a-93b8-0c74d3fe6151"
